\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
section{Formulação teórica
\backslash
label{sec:Formula=0000E7=0000E3o-te=0000F3rica}}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

A determinação das propriedades elásticas efetivas de rochas com múltiplos constituintes minerais exige o uso de modelos de homogeneização que aproximem o comportamento macroscópico do meio composto.
 Neste trabalho,
 são utilizados os modelos de Voigt-Reuss-Hill (VRH) e os limites de Hashin-Shtrikman (HS) para a estimativa do módulo de compressibilidade K e do módulo de cisalhamento G da rocha.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
subsection{Modelo de Voigt-Reuss-Hill (VRH)}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

O modelo de Voigt assume deformação uniforme entre os constituintes da mistura,
 resultando em uma média aritmética ponderada das propriedades dos minerais:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

K_V = 
\backslash
sum_{i=1}^{n} f_i K_i
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

G_V = 
\backslash
sum_{i=1}^{n} f_i G_i
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Já o modelo de Reuss considera tensão uniforme entre os constituintes,
 levando a uma média harmônica ponderada:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
frac{1}{K_R} = 
\backslash
sum_{i=1}^{n} 
\backslash
frac{f_i}{K_i}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
frac{1}{G_R} = 
\backslash
sum_{i=1}^{n} 
\backslash
frac{f_i}{G_i}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

A média de Voigt-Reuss-Hill (VRH) é obtida como a média aritmética simples entre os valores de Voigt e Reuss:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

K_{VRH} = 
\backslash
frac{K_V + K_R}{2}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

G_{VRH} = 
\backslash
frac{G_V + G_R}{2}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
subsection{Limites de Hashin-Shtrikman (HS)}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Os limites de Hashin-Shtrikman oferecem uma estimativa teórica rigorosa dos valores mínimo e máximo possíveis para as propriedades elásticas de materiais isotrópicos compostos por duas ou mais fases.
 Supondo $K_1$ e $G_1$ como os módulos do mineral com maior rigidez e $K_2$ e $G_2$ como os módulos do mineral mais fraco,
 com frações volumétricas $f_1$ e $f_2$,
 os limites são dados por:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

K_{HS}^{+} = K_2 + 
\backslash
frac{f_1}{
\backslash
dfrac{1}{K_1 - K_2} + 
\backslash
dfrac{f_2}{K_2 + 
\backslash
dfrac{4}{3}G_2}}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

G_{HS}^{+} = G_2 + 
\backslash
frac{f_1}{
\backslash
dfrac{1}{G_1 - G_2} + 
\backslash
dfrac{f_2}{G_2 + 
\backslash
zeta}}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

K_{HS}^{-} = K_1 + 
\backslash
frac{f_2}{
\backslash
dfrac{1}{K_2 - K_1} + 
\backslash
dfrac{f_1}{K_1 + 
\backslash
dfrac{4}{3}G_1}}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

G_{HS}^{-} = G_1 + 
\backslash
frac{f_2}{
\backslash
dfrac{1}{G_2 - G_1} + 
\backslash
dfrac{f_1}{G_1 + 
\backslash
zeta'}}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Onde:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
zeta = 
\backslash
frac{G_2(9K_2 + 8G_2)}{6(K_2 + 2G_2)}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
quad ;
 
\backslash
quad
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
zeta' = 
\backslash
frac{G_1(9K_1 + 8G_1)}{6(K_1 + 2G_1)}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{equation}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Esses limites fornecem uma faixa possível para as propriedades efetivas,
 sendo úteis para validar os resultados obtidos com modelos mais simplificados como VRH.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

